  completeExplanation: 'Animals are heterotrophic organisms that acquire biochemical energy and structural materials through nutrition and respiration. Nutrition proceeds through five tightly coordinated phases: Ingestion (intake of solid/liquid food), Digestion (mechanical fragmentation by teeth and enzymatic hydrolysis into soluble monomers), Absorption (translocation of nutrients across the epithelial barrier of the small intestine into blood capillaries), Assimilation (cellular uptake and metabolic utilization of nutrients for biosynthesis and energy), and Egestion (expulsion of unabsorbed fecal residue). In the human digestive tract, digestion initiates in the mouth where salivary amylase converts complex starch into maltose. Peristalsis conveys the bolus through the oesophagus into the stomach, where hydrochloric acid creates an antibacterial acidic environment (pH ~1.5-2) activating pepsin for protein breakdown while mucus protects the gastric mucosa. In the small intestine (~6 metres long), alkaline bile from the liver neutralises stomach acid and emulsifies insoluble fats into micro-droplets, while pancreatic and intestinal enzymes complete carbohydrate, protein, and lipid hydrolysis. Millions of microscopic villi (~0.5 mm) with thin walls and rich capillary beds facilitate rapid diffusion of nutrients. The large intestine reabsorbs water and mineral ions, supported by trillions of beneficial gut bacteria, solidifying waste for egestion. In ruminants (cows), plant cellulose is fermented by symbiotic microorganisms in the rumen and re-chewed as regurgitated cud. Birds rely on a muscular gizzard containing swallowed stones to grind food. Respiration complements nutrition: breathing ventilates the alveolar lungs via pressure differences induced by diaphragm and intercostal muscle contractions, while cellular respiration chemically oxidizes glucose in all cells (Glucose + Oxygen -> Carbon dioxide + Water + Energy). Aquatic fish extract dissolved oxygen via vascularised gills, frogs utilize gills as tadpoles and lungs/cutaneous skin as adults, and earthworms diffuse gases through moist, mucus-covered skin.',
